 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "Musa and Harun: no motif, no figure; Pharaoh and his chiefs not drawn. The staff, the serpent and the hand are not drawn: only light mood and light for «قد جئتكم ببينة». Pharaoh's challenge (106) not glossed (Al-Muyassar's «حسب زعمك» would read as the tafsir's own word). As-Sa'di quotes the verse unbracketed here, so only his own words are used. STAGING v4: Egypt: Pharaoh's court (palace setting, no pyramids). Musa is a blue drop labelled Musa (no motif on his name); Harun is not named in these verses and is not drawn; Pharaoh is a plain faceless king (the label only gives his headdress, it is not drawn); his chiefs three faceless men in dark robes. c1 (103-105): the camera on Pharaoh (103:8) and his chiefs (103:9), who shake their heads on 103:10 (they were unjust with the signs: As-Sa'di, they did not submit to them); an hourglass for see the end of the corrupters (103:15, the drowning is not drawn); Musa speaks (104:1, 105:1), the camera on Pharaoh as he is addressed (104:3, 105:4, 105:15: send the Children of Israel with me; they are not drawn); a seal plate for I came to you with a clear proof (105:11-14), not a light plate, as no light is tied to a miracle word. c2 (106-108): Pharaoh speaks (106:1); his words are a conditional, so no gesture is acted in them, only the camera (106:3, 106:10). The staff, the serpent and the white hand are not drawn and nothing glows or sparkles on the miracle words (107-108); the camera holds on the onlookers (As-Sa'di: while they watched it): the chiefs (107:3), Pharaoh (108:3, Al-Muyassar: a sign for Pharaoh), and wide for the onlookers (108:6). No reaction is added (their answer comes in 109)."
 },
